{"purpose":"Describe walkie's component structure and the single most important structural idea in it — the separation of a control plane from a data plane — so that every decision and work item has a shared picture to attach to.","content":"## Components\n\n```\n            ┌──────────────────────── tailnet (WireGuard) ────────────────────────┐\n            │                                                                     │\n  ┌─────────┴──────────┐                                          ┌───────────────┴────────┐\n  │  client (device A) │                                          │   client (device B)    │\n  │                    │                                          │                        │\n  │  TUI (Bubble Tea)  │                                          │  TUI (Bubble Tea)      │\n  │  local SQLite      │                                          │  local SQLite          │\n  │   history/outbox   │                                          │   history/outbox        │\n  │   TOFU key store   │                                          │   TOFU key store       │\n  │  audio  [tag only] │                                          │  audio  [tag only]     │\n  │  control socket    │                                          │  control socket        │\n  └─────┬────────┬─────┘                                          └────┬─────────────┬─────┘\n        │        │                                                     │             │\n        │        └──────── data plane: direct UDP (voice) / TCP (files) ┘             │\n        │                                                                             │\n        │  control plane: WebSocket + protobuf                                        │\n        └────────────────────────────┐                   ┌──────────────────────────-─┘\n                                     ▼                   ▼\n                        ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                        │  coordinator (tsnet node, container)   │\n                        │                                        │\n                        │  WhoIs auth · presence registry (TTL)  │\n                        │  offline queue (SQLite, pure Go)       │\n                        │  relay fallback data path              │\n                        │  /metrics · /healthz  (tailnet-only)   │\n                        └────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## The coordinator\n\nA single process that joins the tailnet as its own node via tsnet, so it needs no host network privileges and no Tailscale sidecar in its container. It owns exactly what the tailnet does not provide:\n\n- **Identity resolution** — a WhoIs lookup on each accepted connection's remote address. It stores no credentials.\n- **Presence registry** — online state derived from an observed heartbeat, with a liveness TTL.\n- **Offline queue** — per-recipient store-and-forward with monotonic positions and acknowledgement.\n- **Relay fallback** — a data path for peers that cannot establish a direct path to each other.\n- **Observability** — structured logs, metrics and health, bound to the tailnet interface only.\n\nIt deliberately does *not* own: authentication credentials, encryption of direct traffic, NAT traversal, or a user database.\n\n## The client\n\nA single binary. Terminal UI, a control-plane connection to the coordinator, local SQLite for message history and the offline outbox, a trust-on-first-use store for peer public keys, and a local control socket that makes the running client scriptable from the shell.\n\nThe audio module is compiled in only under a build tag. Without it the binary is pure Go and fully static; with it the binary gains capture, encode and playback. Capability is therefore a property of an individual device's build, not of the deployment.\n\n## Two planes, and why the split matters\n\n**Control plane** — presence, text, call setup, queue drain, key distribution. One WebSocket per client to the coordinator, protobuf-framed. Always through the coordinator.\n\n**Data plane** — real-time audio and file bytes. Direct between peers over the tailnet, falling back to a relay through the coordinator when no direct path forms.\n\nThe split exists for one concrete reason: **a control-plane interruption must not tear down an active call.** The WebSocket reconnecting is a routine event; a conversation dropping because of it would not be. The data plane therefore carries its own keepalive and its own lifetime.\n\n## Scale posture\n\nFewer than 20 devices. That number is what licenses a single process, a single SQLite file, no broker, no sharding and no consensus. Every proposal to add one of those must first argue against this number."}